   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the civil service law, in relation to including state university of New York police officers in provisions permitting transfer of police officers",
    "basePrintNo": "S8059",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8059-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §58, Civ Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Civil Service Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S8059\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the civil service law, in relation to including state\nuniversity of New York police officers in provisions permitting transfer\nof police officers\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\nTo grant New York State University police parity with other police\nofficers by allowing them to laterally transfer to other police\nforces or departments listed in section 58(4) of the civil service law.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\nAmends subdivision 3 and paragraphs (a) and (b) of subdivision 4 of\nsection 58 of the civil service law.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nChapter 424 of the laws of 1998 granted police status to SUNY police\nofficers who were at the time peace officers. Under this new\nauthority, these officials are able to make arrests, conduct lawful\nsearches and seizures, execute warrants, investigate crimes, issue\nappearance tickets, operate marked patrol cars, respond in uniform to\ncrimes in progress and enforce all penal and vehicle and traffic laws\nof New York State.\n\nCurrent law does not provide SUNY police the ability to transfer to\nanother police force or department. Section 58 of the civil service\nlaw states that \"any person who has received provisional or permanent\nappointment in the competitive class of the civil service as a police\nofficer of the regional park police or any police force or police\ndepartment of any county, city, town, village, housing authority,\ntransit authority or police district\" shall be eligible to resign and\nbe appointed (i.e. transferred) to another police force or department\nlisted in this section. It further states that the age requirement\nshall be waived provided that \"such second or subsequent appointments\noccurs within 30 days of the date of resignation. Because SUNY police\nofficers are not employed by an entity listed in section 58, there is\nno authority for these police officers to transfer to other forces or\ndepartments listed.\n\nSUNY police are hired from a competitive civil service list,\nestablished using the same civil service test taken by municipal\npolice officers on the same exam dates and locations. SUNY police\nmust pass both health and psychological exams prescribed and\nconducted by the NYS Department of Civil Service. They must pass the\nsame physical tests as municipal police officers, using the Cooper\nStandards, administered by qualified physical fitness examiners\ncertified by the NYS Department of Criminal Justice Services.\n\nThe SUNY police are seeking parity in relation to the benefit granted\nother police officers. This legislation will afford SUNY police\nofficers\nthe opportunity to laterally transfer to another police force or\ndepartment.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n1999-2000 A.8302 - Assembly Governmental Employees\n2001-2002 A.3063/S.1761 - Passed Assembly/Senate Civil Service\n2003-2004 A.1219/S.618 - Passed both Houses, Vetoed by Governor\n2005-2006 A.7627/S.4477 - Passed Assembly/Senate Rules\n2007-2008 A.2176/S.1891 - Assembly Gov Emps/Senate Civil Service\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n\n",
